WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:09.180
Human beings are the only animals
capable of contemplating

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.500
their own mortality,

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.220
and they've been doing that
for thousands of years.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.980
And yet somehow, in the very recent past,

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:27.260
we have lost the practical wisdom
of what happens as people die.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.140
I think that that's a problem.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.900
And if you agree with me
that it is a problem,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.220
then we have to work out
what we're going to do about it.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.420
When she was in her mid-20s, in the 1920s,

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.580
my grandmother was already deeply familiar
with the sequence of events

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.900
that happened to a human person

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.100
as they were coming
to the end of their life.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.980
And that’s because, as a woman --

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:57.980
and it was usually women’s work --

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.940
she was doing what women
had done for centuries,

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:05.180
looking after people
at the very end of their lives,

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.500
in their own beds, in their own homes,

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.620
supported by their own people,

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.180
because hospital had nothing to offer,

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:16.380
once a person was so sick
that their death was imminent.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.700
And yet, when I reached my mid-twenties,

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.260
in the 1980s,

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:29.340
I had none of her wisdom and understanding
and knowledge of dying,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.500
and that was even though I'd just finished
five years of medical school.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.740
As a newly qualified doctor,

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:40.900
I'd spent five years being trained
to stop people from dying.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:44.820
And actually, if a death happened,

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:48.260
it was a thing that was seen
as a medical disaster.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.100
It was a thing that was embarrassing,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.620
it was a thing of which we do not speak.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.900
Why the difference,

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.740
and why within just a couple
of generations?

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.020
Think about what happened to medicine
over the course of the 20th century.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:10.060
It was not worth going to hospital
when you were dying

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.620
in the 1920s.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:16.340
But by the 1960s, '70s, '80s and onwards,

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.420
think of the fantastic progress
that had been made

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.220
so that people who were so sick
that they might die,

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.860
of course we took them to hospital,
because there were antibiotics,

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.060
there were really clever anesthetics

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.820
that allowed surgeons
to spend a long time unpicking things

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.340
during operations.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:37.420
There were new and very sophisticated
treatments for cancers,

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.580
for heart failure, for kidney failure.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.420
There were intensive care units,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.540
there was transplantation of organs,

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.180
some of that pioneered in this very city.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.500
Medical progress was astonishing,

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.500
taking dying people to hospital
very often saved their lives,

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.580
and that is fantastic.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.500
And yet ...

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.220
By taking dying people out of home
and putting them in hospital,

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.020
we changed our understanding
of the process.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.500
We lost our ownership of the process
and we gave it to healthcare ...

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.300
And we forgot what dying looked like.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.380
So, having been qualified
for just over four years,

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.500
I find myself in a new job.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.100
Having originally intended
a career in cancer medicine,

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.780
I’d spent the last four years
choosing to train

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.860
in the places where the most
sick people were.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:40.620
And then I realized that actually,
what was really interesting to me

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:44.740
was the detective journey
of symptom management

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:49.220
and the emotional integration
of feeling well enough

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.340
to live a little bit
during the very end of life,

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.140
and I went to work in a hospice.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.380
But I'd been working
in a big teaching hospital,

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.860
I'd learned a lot of medicine,

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.540
I'd seen a lot of dying.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.580
We had a patient in the hospice.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.780
She was a memorable woman
for many reasons.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:12.140
She had been a member
of the French Resistance

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.300
during the Second World War.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.180
She'd married a British airman,

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.020
she'd come to live in England.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.220
She'd never lost her French accent.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.860
She had a cloud of glorious white hair,

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:28.100
like a halo.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.100
She had piercing brown eyes,

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.780
the kind of gaze that you feel
a person can see your soul.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:38.300
She was self-contained,
she was a little bit aloof.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.060
In fact, she was a little bit scary.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:42.460
(Laughter)

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.780
One day, she told the nurse
who was looking after her

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.060
that she was terrified of dying in agony,

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.660
because if she were to die in agony,
she might despair in God.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.340
And if she were to despair in God,

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.060
as a French Roman Catholic,

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:00.700
her belief was that that
would be a mortal sin,

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.820
so she would not be able to go to heaven,

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:07.020
and heaven was the place she knew
her husband was waiting for her.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:11.580
This was a profound existential distress.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.700
And my boss said, "We need to go
and talk to her, and you should come,

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.620
you'll find this interesting."

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:19.940
I was 26.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:21.140
Do you remember 26?

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.980
It's the last age when you know
that you know everything.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:25.860
(Laughter)

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.580
So I went along,
wondering what I might learn,

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.180
because I thought
I was quite good at pain control.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:33.220
That conversation ...

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.260
changed my life.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.660
It changed my career.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.380
And it's brought me here.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:44.580
Sitting on her bed,
with me on a little footstool

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:49.180
so I can see him and her,
and the nurse sitting on the chair,

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.180
he said to her,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:55.700
"I'm concerned that you've got worries
about what might happen as you're dying."

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.660
And she said yes.
She knew him well, she trusted him.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:00.580
And he said, "I'm sorry to hear that,

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.980
and I wondered whether it might help you

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:07.100
if I described to you what usually happens
as a person is dying."

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:10.980
And I'm sitting on the stool
of all knowledge, thinking,

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:12.260
"You can't tell her that."

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.420
(Laughter)

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.820
Because I’ve seen lots of dying,
and I know they’re all different.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:19.220
And she said, "Yes, please."

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.580
And he said, "I'll describe
what we usually see,

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:23.860
and if it gets too much, you tell me.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:25.500
Promise, I'll stop.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:30.060
The thing that's really
interesting, Sabine,

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.460
is that as people are dying,

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.020
it doesn't really matter
what the illness is they're dying from.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:37.700
The pattern of events is very similar.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.580
We see people becoming
more and more tired.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:45.180
It's harder and harder for them
to find the energy to do things.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:49.940
In fact, they recharge their energy
not so much by eating and drinking,

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:51.260
but by sleeping.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:53.260
And as time goes by,

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:57.420
what we see is that people sleep more
and they're awake less.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.500
And if they want to do
something important,

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:01.500
they should take a snooze before it."

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.700
She nodded, and she got hold of his hand.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:10.940
"As time goes by," he said,
"we see people are asleep for longer,

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:12.740
they're awake for shorter,

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:15.460
and something interesting
we notice that they don’t is,

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:17.820
maybe it's medicine time,
or there's a visitor,

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.060
we need to waken them ...

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.860
For a period, we can't waken them.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.860
They're not just asleep,

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:26.820
they're actually unconscious.

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:30.020
And when they waken,
they tell us they've had a lovely sleep.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.940
It turns out that human beings
don't recognize

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:34.900
when we become unconscious.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:41.420
And so at the very end of somebody's life,
they're not just asleep,

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.500
they're actually deeply unconscious.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:45.260
And when the brain is unconscious,

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.540
the only part of it that's still working

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.900
is the part that's working
their breathing."

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:52.940
By now, she is sitting right up in bed.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:56.900
She's got hold of one of his hands,
and she's stroking it.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.900
She's nodding at everything he says.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.340
And in the meanwhile,
I’m sitting on my stool,

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:09.380
horrified that he seems to be describing
dying to a dying person.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:12.660
And that feels, to me,
to be really not very OK.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:15.620
But she is mesmerized.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:19.420
"By the time the brain
is deeply unconscious,"

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:20.700
he's saying to her now,

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.340
"the only bit that's still working
is the bit that drives the breathing.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:29.420
And so breathing cycles
we don't normally see start to happen.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:31.620
Reflex automatic breathing

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:35.620
cycles from very deep breaths
becoming shallower and shallower,

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.780
and then going back
to the beginning again,

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:41.860
cycles of fast breathing
that gradually become slower,

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:43.700
maybe with pauses,

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:45.740
and then back to the beginning again.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:47.620
The person can't feel their throat.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:51.060
They don't notice if they breathe out
through their voice box,

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:54.420
making a noise families might think
that they're sighing

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:56.020
or groaning, or uncomfortable.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:57.540
We'll always check,

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:00.180
but it's part of this reflex breathing.

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:05.740
Saliva or mouth-cleaning fluid
won't irritate their throat,

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:08.220
it won't make them cough or swallow.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:10.540
They just lie there
with a little pool of fluids,

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:12.420
sometimes in the back of their throat.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:13.740
It's not in the way.

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:15.700
Air is moving in and out of their lungs,

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:18.460
and it bubbles through
that little film of fluid,

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:22.700
but families can mistake that
for drowning or choking.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:24.780
So one of the things
that we'll do, Sabine,

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:27.340
if your nieces and nephews are here,

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:31.860
is we will make sure we explain to them
what is happening to you."

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:37.300
She's stroking his hands, she's nodding.

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:41.060
She is absolutely taking in
everything that he says.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:44.500
And then he says -- and I think,

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:47.460
"Oh my goodness,
he's going to the last breath."

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:54.220
"And then, during, usually,
one of those phases of slow breathing,

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:55.780
there'll be a breath out ...

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:01.500
that just isn't followed
by another breath in.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:05.500
There is nothing special
about the last breath.

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:09.340
It's so not like
on the television or in cinema.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:13.020
There's no rush of pain at the end.
There's no sudden panic.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.340
There's no feeling of fading away.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:17.820
Sometimes, we who work
in palliative care,"

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:21.060
and I've subsequently
discovered this to be true,

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:26.180
"sometimes, we will walk into a room
where a family has been around a person

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:28.020
who is in the act of dying,

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:31.620
and we'll realize the person
has stopped breathing,

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:34.540
and the family hasn't noticed yet,

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:39.220
because the Hollywood finale
that they're waiting for hasn't happened."

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:47.420
She got hold of both of his hands.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.220
She shook them in hers,

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:54.580
and then she pulled his hands
to her face and she kissed them.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:00.020
And then, she closed her eyes,

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:01.820
she lay back on her pillows.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:04.700
I just watched her relax,

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:08.300
and in her own inimitable and aloof way,

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:10.820
she told us that we were
no longer required.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:14.300
(Laughter)

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:18.100
And my boss said to me, “Are you OK?”
And I said, “Yes.” (Voice breaking)

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:22.140
And I went to the kitchen
to blow my nose and dry my eyes,

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:25.420
and think about what just happened.

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:31.100
Two huge ideas exploding in my brain
at the same time.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:35.700
One is "How have I never noticed that?"

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:39.700
That pattern that he has just explained,

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:42.700
I have seen hundreds of times.

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:44.940
But I was the most junior doctor.

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:48.180
It was my job to stop
the person dying, remember?

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:51.220
So I was so busy worrying
about this person's oxygen levels

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:54.460
and that person's pulse,
and this person's kidney function

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:59.340
that I didn't stand back and see
that there's a pattern going on here.

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:05.260
We can describe the process
of ordinary human dying,

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:09.580
and it's as much a process
as the process of giving birth is.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:11.340
It has stages.

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:14.300
We can recognize them,
we can pace ourselves.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:16.660
We can work out where we are in it.

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:21.260
But even more fascinating

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:26.340
was that realization
from watching Sabine's reaction,

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:31.580
that we can describe
ordinary dying to a dying person ...

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:37.140
and it shines the light
of understanding and information

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:39.180
into that dark place

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:42.420
where all their fears
and imagination were at play.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:46.860
I've gone on in my career
in palliative care

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:50.900
to have that conversation
thousands of times, countless times.

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:52.780
I always offer to stop.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:55.980
I've never been stopped.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:01.100
But what happens at the end
is that relaxation,

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:05.900
that moment of, "Well, that isn't
what I was expecting,"

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:09.940
followed almost immediately
by "Can you tell my family that?"

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:13.500
“Can you tell my wife, my husband,
my kids, my parents?

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.660
That’s not what we’re expecting.”

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:17.780
And I think we can do that.

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:23.780
So losing the wisdom really matters.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.460
And we can't leave it
to palliative care people,

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:28.620
or even medical people,

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:32.260
to reclaim that lost wisdom,
one family at a time.

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:36.460
This is a massive
social public health issue.

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:41.460
And I invite everybody
who's listening to step up.

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:45.300
The reason my grandmother
understood about dying

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:48.620
was that she'd seen it
alongside people who knew it,

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:51.820
who described to her,
as the process was happening,

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:53.340
what she was seeing,

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:56.580
so that she would understand
and not be afraid.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:01.460
And it requires all of us who are mortals,

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:04.860
all of us who love other mortals,

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:08.540
to step up, to say, “Enough,

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:12.500
death is not a medical event,
it's a social event.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.820
It's a deeply personal event.

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:17.700
And we can understand it,

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:19.420
we can describe it,

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:21.540
we can console each other.

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:24.940
We can accompany each other.

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.700
We can reclaim dying."

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:29.940
Thank you.

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:33.020
(Applause)

